-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy: 2Q
+
+The cache uses a 2Q eviction policy with two FIFO queues: **Am** (first-use) and **A1** (reused). New entries are admitted to the head of Am. On a cache hit, if the entry is in Am, it is moved to the head of A1; if it is in A1, it is moved to the head of A1 (recency update within the protected queue). When space is needed, eviction scans the tail of Am first; only when Am is exhausted does eviction scan the tail of A1. This protects repeatedly accessed comparison sheets from being evicted by a long sequential screening that fills Am with one-use entries.
+
+Queue sizing: Am is allocated 25% of the cache capacity (3 GiB), A1 receives the remaining 75% (9 GiB). The split is a runtime parameter stored in the worker's configuration and may be adjusted without code changes. Admission and promotion decisions are made at the granularity of individual preview keys (clip ID + revision + renderer version). All eviction metadata stays private to the preview worker; no other node inspects queue membership, access counts, or recency timestamps.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue FIFO with Am/A1) | LRU; Frequency-based with aging | 2Q protects the small working set of comparison sheets during long sequential screenings. LRU evicts useful comparison sheets during scans. Frequency+aging adds counter maintenance and lags on project switches. 2Q's extra bookkeeping is accepted to protect the reuse pattern. |
+| Queue size split | Am 25% / A1 75% | 50/50; 10/90; adaptive | 25/75 reserves most capacity for protected entries while giving Am enough room to absorb screening bursts. Adaptive splits add control-loop complexity without clear benefit for the pilot's 12 GiB budget. The split is a config parameter for later tuning. |
